Chicago has its own deposit rules that are stricter than the state law, and those are probably the ones that apply to you. The guide I checked covers the Illinois state law in detail but doesn't give the specific Chicago deadlines or penalty amounts.

**Illinois state rules (Security Deposit Return Act, 765 ILCS 710)**
- **Deadlines:** Within **30 days** after you move out, the landlord must send an itemized list of deductions, with receipts or cost estimates. If they don't send a valid list, they must return the **full deposit within 45 days**.
- **Who it covers:** Since January 1, 2024, the law applies to every residential landlord. The old rule exempting buildings with fewer than 5 units was removed.
- **Penalty:** If a landlord acts in **bad faith** by refusing to itemize, inventing charges or not returning the deposit on time, they can owe **twice the deposit**, plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees.
- **Interest:** Buildings with 25 or more units must pay interest on deposits held for six months or longer (765 ILCS 715).
- **Deposit size:** There's no statewide limit.

**Chicago's rules (RLTO § 5-12-080)**
Most Chicago rentals fall under the city's Residential Landlord and Tenant Ordinance (RLTO). It has its own rules on receipts, interest and a 2x penalty, and is stricter than the state law. The main exception is an owner-occupied building with six or fewer units. The guide doesn't list the RLTO's specific deadlines or penalty math, so I won't guess at them. Read § 5-12-080 itself or ask a Chicago tenant organization. The City of Chicago's page on deposit interest rates is here: https://www.chicago.gov/city/en/depts/doh/provdrs/landlords/svcs/security-deposit-interest-rates.html

**What to do next**
- Check whether your building is exempt (owner lives there and it has six or fewer units). That tells you whether the Chicago rules or only the state rules apply.
- Collect your evidence: the landlord's list of deductions, your lease, the date you moved out, and any dated photos from moving in and out.
- If the charges seem made up and lack receipts or estimates, that may show the bad faith that triggers the 2x penalty.

**Sources:** PropInspect's Illinois guide, https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/illinois, last reviewed September 18, 2026. State law text: https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/legislation/ilcs/documents/076507100K1.htm

This is general information, not legal advice. For your specific situation, talk to an Illinois landlord-tenant lawyer or a local tenant rights group. Because the penalty includes attorney's fees, some lawyers will take cases like this.